    tags: ["guide", "miljodata-2025-data-breach"],
    body: `Over the weekend of August 23-24, 2025, a ransomware gang tracked as Datacarry broke into the systems of Miljödata, a Swedish software company whose HR and case-management platform, Adato, is used by roughly 80 percent of Sweden's municipalities and regions to manage employee records, sick leave, and workplace-rehabilitation cases. Miljödata disclosed the intrusion on August 25 and said the attackers had stolen data and were demanding a ransom of about 1.5 bitcoin to keep it from being published. Miljödata did not pay, and on September 13 the stolen files showed up on the group's dark-web leak site — turning what started as a single vendor breach into one of the largest data exposures in Swedish history.

Because Adato is embedded in municipal HR systems rather than customer-facing ones, the people affected weren't Miljödata's own customers — they were roughly 1.5 million public-sector employees and residents whose data those municipalities and regions had entered into the platform on their behalf. Around 200 municipalities and regions were affected, including Stockholm, Gothenburg, Gotland, Halland, Kalmar, Karlstad, Umeå, Luleå, and Skellefteå. The stolen data included full names, phone numbers, home addresses, dates of birth, and personnummer — the Swedish personal identity number used to authenticate almost everything in daily Swedish life, from banking and healthcare to tax filings and BankID logins. For some individuals, the leak went further, exposing sensitive details from workplace sick-leave and rehabilitation cases, including medical certificates and descriptions of occupational injuries.

A personnummer is a far more powerful piece of data than it might sound. Unlike a U.S. Social Security number, which is meant to stay behind the scenes, Sweden's personnummer is used openly and constantly as an identifier — which is exactly why a leaked one, combined with a real name, address, and phone number, is valuable to a scammer. The most direct risk is vishing: someone calling and claiming to be from a bank, government agency, or employer, citing real personal details stolen in the breach to sound credible, and then pressuring the target to approve a BankID authentication request or read back a one-time code — at which point the scammer can drain a bank account or take out a loan in the victim's name. Sweden's privacy regulator, the Authority for Privacy Protection (IMY), announced on November 3, 2025 that it had opened formal GDPR investigations into Miljödata and three of the public bodies that used its platform — the City of Gothenburg, the Municipality of Älmhult, and the Region of Västmanland — over how the data was handled.

If your municipality or employer has confirmed you were affected, or if you work for a Swedish public-sector employer that used Miljödata's Adato system, a few concrete precautions matter most. Never approve a BankID request or read out a verification code because someone who called or texted you asked you to — if you're unsure whether a contact is legitimate, hang up and call the organization back using a number you already know, not one the caller gives you. Treat any unsolicited call, text, or email that cites your personnummer, a recent sick-leave case, or other personal detail and then asks you to "verify" it as a probable scam. Keep an eye on your credit reports and any notifications from Swedish credit bureaus (UC, Bisnode) for a loan or account you didn't open, and consider registering for a kreditupplysningsspärr (credit-check block) with your bank if you're especially concerned. Affected individuals can report suspected fraud to the Swedish Police at polisen.se and raise data-protection concerns with IMY at imy.se; residents elsewhere who receive a suspicious message referencing this breach can report it to their own national data-protection authority or, in the U.S., to the FTC at ReportFraud.ftc.gov.`,
