    tags: ["guide", "eecu-educational-employees-credit-union-2026-data-breach"],
    body: `Educational Employees Credit Union (EECU), a Fresno-based credit union that serves more than 400,000 members across 12 California counties and bills itself as the largest locally based credit union in the San Joaquin Valley, began notifying members in late May 2026 that a data breach dating back to the previous December had exposed their personal information. EECU says it learned on December 15, 2025 that an unauthorized individual had gained access to a single employee's email account for a limited period. The credit union secured the account and brought in outside cybersecurity investigators, but a full review of what was actually inside that mailbox took months — EECU didn't determine that the exposed emails contained members' sensitive personal information until May 8, 2026, nearly five months after the intrusion was first identified, with notification letters going out roughly three weeks after that.

EECU has not published a total number of affected members, but state breach-notification filings give a partial picture of the reach: at least 949 people in Texas and 34 in Massachusetts were among those notified, meaning the breach reached members well outside EECU's home base in California's Central Valley. The exposed information varies by individual but includes, in different combinations, full names, home addresses, Social Security numbers, driver's license numbers, and financial account information — exactly the combination identity thieves need to open new credit accounts, file fraudulent tax returns, or take out loans in someone else's name.

The mechanism behind this breach is worth pausing on, because it doesn't involve a sophisticated hack of EECU's core banking systems, a ransomware gang, or a vulnerability in any software product — it involves one employee's email inbox. Financial institutions route enormous amounts of sensitive member correspondence, loan documents, and account paperwork through ordinary email over the course of daily business, and a single compromised employee account can sit on months or years of that traffic. That makes routine business email one of the highest-value, lowest-defended targets a bank or credit union has, since it doesn't require defeating encryption or breaching a database — it just requires getting into one person's mailbox, whether through a phishing email, a reused password, or a social-engineering call.

EECU is offering affected members two years of complimentary identity monitoring, credit monitoring, fraud consultation, and identity theft restoration services through Kroll, and the credit union has said it has no evidence so far that the exposed information has actually been used to commit fraud. That's a meaningful offer worth signing up for, but "no evidence yet" is not the same as "safe" — stolen Social Security numbers and driver's license numbers don't expire, and criminals sometimes sit on stolen data for months before using it, which is exactly why EECU's own gap between detecting the intrusion and confirming what was exposed matters: members had no way to protect themselves during the months the credit union was still investigating.

If you're an EECU member, enroll in the free Kroll monitoring services referenced in your notification letter, and independently place a security freeze on your credit reports with Equifax, Experian, and TransUnion — a freeze is free, stronger protection than monitoring alone, and isn't limited to two years. Watch your EECU accounts and any other financial accounts for unfamiliar activity, and keep your breach notification letter along with any related correspondence in case you need it later to document your case. Treat any unexpected call, text, or email that references EECU, your account, or "compensation" for this breach with suspicion — verify by contacting EECU directly through myeecu.org or a phone number from a past statement, never through a link or number provided in the message itself. If you believe your identity has been stolen, report it at IdentityTheft.gov, and report any phishing attempt referencing this breach to the FTC at ReportFraud.ftc.gov.`,
