    coverImage: "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Hurricane_Melissa_at_landfall_true_color.png?width=1200",
    coverImageCredit: "NASA (public domain) — satellite image of Hurricane Melissa at landfall in Jamaica, October 2025",
    coverImagePosition: 50,
    body: `Hurricane Melissa made landfall in Jamaica at the end of October 2025 as a catastrophic Category 5 storm, one of the strongest hurricanes ever recorded to hit the island, devastating homes, the power grid, and infrastructure across large stretches of the country. Within hours of the first news coverage, fraud researchers and Jamaica's own Cyber Incident Response Team (Ja-CIRT) began documenting a wave of fake donation websites built to intercept the money people wanted to send to help.

Netcraft, a cybersecurity firm that tracks online fraud, identified at least 14 fraudulent domains built around the disaster in the days that followed, including sites like "donate.melissarelieffundtojamaicans.online," "jamaicahurricanerelief.com," "melissareliefjamaica.com," and "supportjamaicagovjm.com" — names deliberately chosen to sound official or to mimic government and established charity branding. Jamaica's Cyber Incident Response Team separately flagged more than two dozen fraudulent relief websites in an official public warning, urging donors to give only through verified government and established charity channels. Some of these fake sites cloned the visual design and text of real humanitarian organizations wholesale, making them difficult to distinguish from the real thing at a glance. Investigators also found scammers using AI-generated images and video to make fabricated damage photos and "victim" appeals feel more emotionally urgent and convincing.

One tactic worth understanding specifically: several fraudulent sites displayed a static QR code linked to a cryptocurrency wallet address, rather than a normal payment form. Scammers favor cryptocurrency for exactly this reason — once a donation is sent, the transaction can't be reversed or clawed back the way a credit card chargeback can, and the wallet itself offers no way to verify who controls it or whether any of the money will reach Jamaica at all. A wallet address with no visible history of legitimate transaction activity, sitting behind a QR code on a donation page, is a strong warning sign on its own.

This pattern repeats after essentially every major disaster — hurricanes, wildfires, earthquakes — because scammers know that people's instinct to help outpaces their instinct to verify. Before donating to any disaster relief effort, go directly to a charity's known web address by typing it yourself rather than clicking a link from an email, text, or social media post, and check the organization's standing through an independent evaluator like the BBB Wise Giving Alliance or Charity Navigator. Favor donation methods that offer some recourse, such as a credit card, over methods that don't, including wire transfers, gift cards, or cryptocurrency. Be skeptical of newly registered domains, generic "relief fund" names not tied to a specific known organization, and any appeal that pressures you to give immediately without giving you time to check it out. If you spot a suspicious disaster-relief solicitation, you can report it to the FTC at ReportFraud.ftc.gov; Jamaican residents can report directly to Ja-CIRT through the Office of the Prime Minister's website.`,
